Steve Fenter <steven.fenter58@gmail.com> writes:=0A=
=0A=
>I've done a fair amount of TLS handshake troubleshooting, and it's usually=
=0A=
>long and painful because the error codes are so vague. =0A=
>[...]=0A=
>The vague error messages are leading directly to more downtime, and this=
=0A=
>should be balanced with the other security needs. =0A=
=0A=
This was the reason for the sole new feature that was added to SCEP, an=0A=
optional text-form error message to explain why you didn't get a certificat=
e.=0A=
Prior to that it was pure guesswork, there was just a generic error code=0A=
saying "you didn't get your cert", which made things almost impossible to=
=0A=
debug if you didn't have someone you could phone at the CA who could tell y=
ou=0A=
why you didn't get your cert.=0A=
=0A=
As you mention, debugging TLS is unnecessarily painful if there's a problem=
,=0A=
you typically just get a handshake-failed alert which is essentially no=0A=
information at all.  Having a debug-mode capability to send back a long-for=
m=0A=
error message would be extremely useful, maybe an extension to say "send ba=
ck=0A=
a long-form alert with more than just 'BOOLEAN succeeded =3D FALSE' in it".=
=0A=
=0A=
Peter.=0A=

